  "verbalization": "Chlorambucil is linked to Hematologic Cancer through a series of indirect pathways, primarily through its role as an antineoplastic agent. The evidence suggests that Chlorambucil exerts its effects via alkylating activity, which is shared with compounds like Lomustine and Thiotepa, both of which are known to treat Hematologic Cancer. This indicates a potential mechanism of action involving DNA damage, a common feature of alkylating agents. The lowest common ancestor of Chlorambucil and these compounds is the antineoplastic alkylating agent class, reinforcing the connection to cancer treatment. The evidence also highlights that Chlorambucil is associated with aromatic amine and organochlorine compounds, which may contribute to its cytotoxic effects. While the direct causal link between Chlorambucil and Hematologic Cancer is not explicitly stated, the cumulative evidence suggests a plausible mechanism through which Chlorambucil may contribute to the development of hematologic malignancies.",
